  "body": "Part of #904. Found while working #904: swarf is the one multi-axis family still refused on a reduced-fidelity model.\n\n**What is owed.** A swarf tool cuts with its flank. That the flank stands clear of the wall is proved only inside the generator (`motion` `multiaxis/stamp_pose.rs` and `stamp_rise.rs`, through `stamp_link::flank_clear` at the plan tolerance). No verify law re-measures it: law (f) judges the ball, and the reach laws judge the bands above the flute. So on a model imported coarser than promised there is no verdict to tighten, and `operation::fidelity_law::excess_aware` keeps Swarf and Advanced Swarf refused at the program gate.\n\nTwo ways to close it, to be decided by whoever takes it:\n\n1. Thread the model's excess into the flank proof (`Ruled::tol` as `flank_clear` reads it), so the generator proves clearance with the tightened tolerance. This changes the paths generated on a reduced-fidelity model.\n2. Add a verify law that measures the flank against the part, and tighten that. Larger, but it would also judge the flank at full fidelity, where today only the generator speaks.\n\n**Done means:** Swarf and Advanced Swarf on a reduced-fidelity model are judged while a positive tolerance is left (and refused by name past that), with a pin for a flank cut between the tightened and the plain tolerance; or the refusal stays with the reason recorded in `docs/decisions`.\n",
